The dispatcher, v1 to v2

The companion repository keeps, alongside the paper, the machinery that actually ran the experimental dispatches, because reproducing the record means being able to reproduce how the numbers got made. The heart of that machinery is a small shell script, and the difference between its first version and its second is the difference between believing a worker did something and knowing that it did. This section walks through what changed, why it changed, and what the current script guarantees about every row it writes. The point is not to defend the script but to make its behavior legible: if you run it, you should be able to tell, from the files it leaves behind, exactly what happened on each dispatch, down to whether the answer the worker was supposed to produce actually appeared.

The first version of the dispatch script, written as it was run and kept under the name v1, had a design that looked reasonable on paper and failed in a way that only became obvious once the workers were real. Its structure was: write the instrument row first, then run the acceptance test, then fill in the result. That ordering mattered, because it meant the row existed before the test had a chance to fail, and the record could end up carrying a verdict the test had never actually delivered. The verdict itself was coarse: the script recorded the exit status of the worker process, printing OK if the process exited with status zero and TIMEOUT if it did not. The thing the whole exercise was supposed to be about, the needle outcome, was not logged at all. So a reader of a v1 dispatch file could learn that a process finished, never that the worker produced the required answer.

The second trouble with v1 was how it invoked the worker. It called the command-line interface in a quiet-ish mode, but not the quietest one available. On that invocation path, the CLI echoes the full brief to standard output before the machine starts talking, then boxes its response, then appends an exit summary. The net effect is that the transcript is big and full of the very text that was fed in. This mattered because every brief, by construction, ended with a trailer naming its needle; that trailer was part of the prompt, and therefore part of the echo. The acceptance test in v1 searched the whole transcript for evidence that the needle had been produced. Because the echo reproduced the brief, including its needle trailer, the test could be satisfied by the echo alone, before the worker had contributed anything. That is the incident numbered INC-008 in the record: a pass that meant nothing, a false green on a test that could not distinguish the machine restating its instructions from the machine actually following them. The row was written, the status was OK, and what the row said was not what the run meant.

There was a quieter, more mechanical defect lurking in the same period. The command-line tool's terminal library, when it writes to a file rather than a terminal, uses CRLF line endings. The test that exercised this behavior used a hand-written fixture, and whoever wrote it by hand did not include the carriage return. So the fixture did not represent what the real tool emitted, and the test quietly passed against a string it would never actually see. That gap was closed by changing how fixtures are produced: they are now generated by the same print call the real run uses, which automatically includes a CRLF case. In other words, the test now fights the real output, not a cleaned-up copy of it.

The second version of the script addresses the first two defects directly, and the third one indirectly, because it changes what the script considers acceptable evidence. The invocation changed from the echo-heavy path to a truly minimal one: the flag is now -Q, which is a flag in its own right and does not replace the way the brief is passed; the brief still goes through -q. On that path the CLI prints the final response alone, and it prints the session id to standard error rather than mixing it into the transcript. That single change defuses INC-008 at the source, because there is no longer any echo of the brief in the stream the test inspects; the only text available is the worker's actual response. The transcript is no longer a confusion of prompts and echoes, and the needle trailer is no longer present unless the worker itself chose to reproduce it.

The acceptance logic was rebuilt around that cleaner stream. It normalizes carriage returns, so the CRLF behavior of the terminal library no longer matters to the outcome. It requires the needle to appear as the last non-empty line of the response, which is a much stricter condition than "somewhere in the transcript," and it is the condition that actually corresponds to the task being done. The row is no longer written before the gate; it is written after the acceptance test runs, so a row in the file means the gate ran. The order of operations was inverted from v1, and that ordering is now part of the contract.

The verdict column carries more information in v2. Instead of the binary OK or TIMEOUT, it can be OK, NEEDLE-MISS, TIMEOUT, or ERROR, and it is accompanied by the numeric exit code of the worker process. Alongside that go the byte count of the brief, the number of bytes printed back to the hub, and a per-dispatch key that ties each row to the retained .out and .err files, so any single dispatch can be followed from its row to its raw output and back. Locking was reworked for the platform the script actually runs on: it uses mkdir as the locking primitive, because flock is not available on macOS, and when a lock cannot be acquired it writes a pending file rather than silently proceeding or failing.

There has been exactly one live v2 dispatch so far, and it behaved as the design intends: it ran in nine seconds, returned a response of 449 bytes to the hub, and the needle was hit. It is a single run, not a demonstration at scale, but it is a run on the real path with the real gate, and it is the run that the current file format and the current locking are built to represent.

Finally, the row policy distinguishes two very different kinds of failure, because they deserve different records. A worker process that exits non-zero after it has actually been executed is a worker that ran and then fell over; that is written as an ERROR row, and its files are kept, because the failure is informative and the output may explain it. By contrast, a failure that happens before any worker is executed at all, a script precondition failure, is treated as a different animal: a missing or unreadable brief, the kanban variable already set, hermes not on the PATH, or an unknown worker name. In those cases the script writes no row and exits with status two. The reasoning is that no dispatch has happened, so there is nothing to record as a dispatch; a row in the file represents a run that was attempted, and a precondition failure never got that far. Keeping the two categories separate means a reader of the record can tell, without reading the code, whether a given absence means a worker failed or a worker was never started.

What the v2 script gives the reproducing reader, then, is a chain that v1 could not provide. Every row corresponds to a gate that actually ran, every verdict is backed by a numeric exit code and a session id, every dispatch's raw output is reachable through its key, the needle condition is strict and unambiguous, and failures are pigeonholed into kinds that mean something. The single live run is modest, but the mechanism around it closes the specific gap that INC-008 exposed: the gap between the record claiming a pass and the record being able to show how the pass was obtained. If you reproduce this repository's runs, the files tell you what each one means, and no row in them will have been written before its test had a chance to speak.

